   "item": {
    "question_text": "A model-training platform is reached through a single login, \"ds-admin\", that four data engineers share and that carries administrative rights. One of those engineers moved to the finance department six weeks ago; his own named account on the platform is still enabled, although he no longer works with the platform. Applying identity-lifecycle reasoning, which course of action best fits the arrangement?",
    "options": [
     {
      "text": "Retain the shared login only where an operational need for it has been specifically approved and recorded, and disable the mover's named account promptly.",
      "is_correct": true
     },
     {
      "text": "Keep the shared login unchanged provided its password is rotated after each maintenance session, and leave the mover's account until the annual access review.",
      "is_correct": false
     },
     {
      "text": "Issue each engineer a second platform identity alongside the shared one, and keep the mover's account live so his earlier reports stay reachable.",
      "is_correct": false
     },
     {
      "text": "Reclassify the shared login as a service identity so that no approval is needed for it, and hand the mover's account over to his new finance manager.",
      "is_correct": false
     }
